/-!
# `Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧` for an arbitrary object, via the Koszul Hom towers

[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] asserts that `Rf_*` commutes with derived completion,
`Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧`, for an **arbitrary** `K ∈ D(𝒪')`, and [Stacks, Tag 0A0I] writes that
identity down as Equation 52.7.0.1. This file proves the identity for an arbitrary `K` at a pair
of sheaves of ideals generated by finitely many global sections — the regime the source's own
proof reduces to, and the regime in which [Stacks, Tag 099F] is available in this library, so
that both derived completion functors are built out of the generators and no part of that tag is
an input.

The proof follows the source's argument, in the **Hom tower** rather than the tensor tower:

1. `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_derivedSheafHomSystem_derivedCompletion` — the
   Hom-tower form of [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] — presents `K^∧` as the derived limit of
   `n ↦ R𝓗om_{𝒪'}(K(𝒪', g_1^{n+1}, …, g_r^{n+1}), K)`;
2. `Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedPushforwardOnRingedTopoi.isDerivedLimit_map_derivedPushforward`
   ([Stacks, Tag 0A07]) carries that derived limit through `Rf_*`;
3. the pushed-forward tower is identified with the tower of the target by
   `derivedPushforwardKoszulSheafHomSystemIso` below;
4. step 1 again, at `Rf_*K` on the target, presents `(Rf_*K)^∧` as the derived limit of the
   target tower, and `Rlim.Pro.nonempty_iso_of_isDerivedLimit_of_natIso` ([Stacks, Tag 0H9L])
   concludes.

## Why the Hom tower

The engine `nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf_of_natIso` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionKoszulTower.lean` runs the same four steps over the tensor
tower `n ↦ K ⊗^𝐋 K(𝒪', g^{n+1})^∨`, and takes its step 3 as a hypothesis `hproj`: identifying
that tower with the target's needs the projection formula [Stacks, Tag 0944] in invertible form.

At the Hom tower, step 3 needs no projection formula at all. It is
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedInternalHomPushforward.derivedPushforwardSheafHomIsoOfObj`,

`Rf_* R𝓗om_{𝒪'}(Lf^*A, M) ≅ R𝓗om_𝒪(A, Rf_*M)`,

which holds for arbitrary `A ∈ D(𝒪)` and `M ∈ D(𝒪')` — both sides are right adjoints of
`N ↦ Lf^*A ⊗^𝐋_{𝒪'} Lf^*N` — whiskered with the Koszul tower of the target and composed with
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedPullbackKoszulPowerSystemIso`,
`Lf^*K(𝒪, t^{n+1}) ≅ K(𝒪', g^{n+1})`. Step 4 consumes a **natural** isomorphism of towers, so
the naturality of the adjoint formula in `A` is part of the input; that is why
`derivedPushforwardSheafHomIsoOfObj`, which is cut from the bifunctor form
`derivedPushforwardSheafHomBifunctorIso`, is the piece used rather than the one-variable
`derivedPushforwardSheafHomIso`.

The two towers are not interchangeable: passing between `K ⊗^𝐋 K(𝒪', g^{n+1})^∨` and
`R𝓗om_{𝒪'}(K(𝒪', g^{n+1}), K)` is clause 3 of [Stacks, Tag 08JJ].

## Main definitions

* `derivedPushforwardKoszulSheafHomSystemIso`: the isomorphism of towers
  `R𝓗om_{𝒪'}(K(𝒪', g^{n+1}), K) ⋙ Rf_* ≅ R𝓗om_𝒪(K(𝒪, t^{n+1}), Rf_*K)` in `D(𝒪)`, natural in
  `n`, which is step 3.

## Main results

* `nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf`:
  **`Rf_*(K^∧) ≅ (Rf_*K)^∧` for an arbitrary `K`**, at `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_r) ⊂ 𝒪` and
  `ℐ' = (g_1, …, g_r) ⊂ 𝒪'` with `g_i = f^♯(t_i)`.
* `nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_pushGlobalSections`: the same at the
  source's own ideal sheaf `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, built from `ℐ` rather than given, available
  when the target site has a terminal object whose image is terminal.

## Implementation notes

*The hypothesis on the data.* One property of the data enters, visible in the signatures:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushGlobalSectionsCompatible f t g`, that `g_i` is `f^♯(t_i)` on
sections over every object of the target site. This is the source's own relation between the
two generating families — "Then `ℐ'` is generated by `g_i = f^♯(f_i)`" — and in the second
statement it is discharged from the data, `g` being `pushGlobalSections f hT' t`. It is a
hypothesis on the *data* and not on the object: `K` ranges over all of `D(𝒪')`. Invertibility of
the comparison `f^*𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'` is not assumed: it holds at every morphism of ringed sites,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_pullbackObjUnitToUnit_fSharpRing`, by [Stacks, Tag 00X1].

*Objects, not the canonical map.* The conclusion is the display as the source writes it,
`Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧`, an isomorphism of the two objects. It is not the assertion that
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison` is invertible: the
uniqueness of derived limits is not canonical, so the isomorphism produced in step 4 is not
known to be that map. The same remark governs the tensor-tower engine, and for the same reason.

*The relation between the two ideal sheaves.* `PushforwardIdealCompatible`, which the
tensor-tower engine records, does not appear here. The derived-limit argument does not use it,
and `PushGlobalSectionsCompatible` is the stronger and more primitive statement — the one the
Koszul pullback needs.

*The countable products.* Every `Limits.HasProduct` needed is produced inside the proofs by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.hasProductsOfShape_nat_derivedCategory_ringSheaf`, from
K-injective resolutions, so no exactness of countable products is assumed. The
`Limits.HasCoproduct` arguments are instance binders, as they are on [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] itself.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0944](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0944)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099F)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08JJ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08JJ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A07](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A07)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0E](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0E)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0G](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0G)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0I](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0I)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0H9L](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H9L)
-/

